virtual-particle-factor-7f505b86.conceptual_definition:
The formula provided, 
\text{Propagator} = \frac{i}{p^2 - m^2 + i\epsilon}
, is not a direct definition of a 'Virtual Particle Factor' but rather represents the Feynman propagator for a scalar particle in quantum field theory. It quantifies the amplitude for a particle to travel between two spacetime points, considering the probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics and the concept of virtual particles as intermediate states in interactions.

the-equation-of-motion-8c314245.symmetry_origin:
This equation is a direct consequence of the principle of conservation of momentum. If the net force acting on a system is zero (

$$ \mathbf{F} = 0 $$

), then the time derivative of momentum is also zero (

$$ \dot{\mathbf{p}} = 0 $$

), implying that the momentum of the system remains constant. This is the statement of conservation of momentum. This equation can also be derived from Newton's second law of motion in its more common form, 

$$ \mathbf{F} = m\mathbf{a} $$

, by substituting the definition of acceleration as the time derivative of velocity (

$$ \mathbf{a} = \dot{\mathbf{v}} $$

) and assuming constant mass (

$$ \mathbf{F} = m\dot{\mathbf{v}} = \dot{m\mathbf{v}} = \dot{\mathbf{p}} $$

). However, the form 

$$ \mathbf{F} = \dot{\mathbf{p}} $$

is more general as it holds even when the mass of the system is not constant (e.g., rockets expelling fuel).
